.\" Man page for safe-power.  View locally:  man ./safe-power.8
.TH SAFE-POWER 8 "2026-09-30" "safe-power 2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
safe-power \- drain, reboot or shut down a Kubernetes/Docker node safely, and restore it on boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B safe-power
.RB { \-\-reboot | \-\-shutdown | \-\-drain | \-\-restore | \-\-status | \-\-check }
.RI [ NODE ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B safe-power
.RB { \-\-reboot | \-\-shutdown | \-\-status | \-\-check }
.B \-\-all
.RB [ \-\-workers-only ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B safe-power-setup
.RI [ options ]
.IR NODE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B safe-power
takes a node out of service cleanly before powering it off, and puts it back
automatically when it boots. It can be run on any node of the cluster and can
target any node, or roll through the whole cluster one node at a time.
.PP
When run as a normal user it re-executes itself through
.BR sudo (8).
.PP
The node's role is taken from its short hostname: the host named by
.B CONTROL_PLANE_HOST
is the control plane, and every other host is a worker.
.SH PHASES
A power action runs these phases in order. If any phase fails before the
power action, the node is left running and in service.
.TP
.B 1. Preflight
Aborts if
.BR apt / dpkg
is running.
.TP
.B 2. Backup detection
Looks for a backup in progress, anywhere on the node:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
processes named in
.B BACKUP_PROCS
(restic, borg, kopia, vzdump, veeamagent, pg_dump, mysqldump, ...), including
processes running inside containers, which are reported with the container
name. Long-running daemons that match
.B BACKUP_IGNORE
(for example
.IR "kopia server" ,
.IR "restic mount" )
are ignored;
.IP \(bu 2
command lines matching
.B BACKUP_CMDLINE
(backup scripts such as
.IR backup.sh );
.IP \(bu 2
systemd services matching
.B BACKUP_UNITS
that are still running;
.IP \(bu 2
containers named in
.B BLOCKING_CONTAINERS
(one-shot backup jobs).
.RE
.IP
By default a running backup aborts the run.
.B \-\-wait-backups
waits for it to finish (up to one hour, or
.B BACKUP_WAIT
seconds), and
.B \-\-ignore-backups
continues anyway.
.TP
.B 3. Container discovery
Lists every running Docker container this node must stop itself, and puts
each one into a shutdown tier. Swarm tasks are left to the Swarm drain,
Kubernetes-managed containers are left to
.BR "kubectl drain" ,
and containers labelled
.B safe-power.skip=true
are left alone.
.RS
.TP 10
.B tier 1
apps: everything not listed below.
.TP
.B tier 2
data: databases and brokers, matched against
.BR DB_IMAGES .
.TP
.B tier 3
infra: DNS, reverse proxies, VPN and management tools, matched against
.BR INFRA_IMAGES .
.RE
.IP
Matching uses the image's own name without registry, owner or tag, so
.I bitnami/postgresql:16
is data but
.I redis_exporter
is an app. The container label
.BR safe-power.tier=1 | 2 | 3
overrides the guess.
.TP
.B 4. Kubernetes drain
First it takes the one-node-down lock: the node cordons itself, then checks
whether any other node is cordoned or
.BR NotReady .
If one is, it uncordons itself and aborts. The read requests the most recent
Kubernetes state. Competing nodes cannot both pass while both remain
cordoned; either or both may back off. A failed cordon or node-list request,
or a read that does not confirm this node is cordoned, aborts before drain.
If rollback cannot uncordon the node, its drained state is kept for recovery
with
.BR \-\-restore .
Turn this check off with
.B ONE_AT_A_TIME=0
or skip it once with
.BR \-\-allow-concurrent .
.IP
Then it runs
.B kubectl drain
.I NODE
.BR "\-\-ignore-daemonsets \-\-delete-emptydir-data" .
If the drain fails (for example because of a PodDisruptionBudget or a stuck
pod), the node is uncordoned and the run aborts without powering off. A worker
with no
.B kubectl
access refuses to continue.
.IP
During a cluster shutdown
.RB ( "\-\-shutdown \-\-all" )
the node is only cordoned: pods are not evicted because there is nowhere for
them to go, and there is no lock check.
.TP
.B 5. Docker shutdown
Drains the Swarm node if Swarm is active. Then it stops the local containers
tier by tier: apps, then data, then infra. Apps and infra get
.B STOP_TIMEOUT
seconds to stop; data gets
.B DB_STOP_TIMEOUT
seconds. A container still running afterwards, or one that had to be killed
(exit code 137), is reported.
.TP
.B 6. Power
Enables the one-shot unit
.BR safe-power-restore.service ,
runs
.BR sync ,
then reboots or powers off.
.B \-\-drain
stops here without a power action.
.TP
.B 7. Restore
Runs at the next boot, or when started with
.BR \-\-restore :
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Sets the Swarm node active again.
.IP \(bu 2
Starts the containers that were running, tier by tier in reverse (infra,
then data, then apps). Before starting the next tier it waits up to
.B HEALTH_WAIT
seconds for containers with a healthcheck to report healthy.
.IP \(bu 2
Waits for the Kubernetes node to be
.BR Ready ,
then runs
.BR "kubectl uncordon" .
.IP \(bu 2
Waits for Swarm services to converge. On the control plane it also checks
.BR HEALTH_URLS .
.RE
.IP
When done, the unit removes itself and the result is recorded for
.BR \-\-status .
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-reboot
Phases 1\(en6 followed by a reboot; phase 7 runs on boot.
.TP
.B \-\-shutdown
Phases 1\(en6 followed by a power-off; phase 7 runs at the next power-on.
.TP
.B \-\-drain
Phases 1\(en5 only. Undo with
.BR \-\-restore .
.TP
.B \-\-restore
Phase 7.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Prints one of
.BR "drained since" ...,
.BR "ok restored" ...,
or
.BR failed ...
.TP
.B \-\-check
Checks the node's setup:
.BR cluster.conf ,
the service account, its SSH key, the sudoers rule,
.B kubectl
access, and SSH access to every other node.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I NODE
Run the action on
.I NODE
instead of this host. The calling node connects over SSH as the service
account and runs
.I NODE's
own
.BR safe-power .
For
.BR \-\-reboot ,
the caller waits until
.I NODE
reports that its restore is ok or failed.
.TP
.B \-\-all
With
.BR \-\-reboot :
a rolling reboot. First it checks that every node is reachable, that none is
already drained, and that every Kubernetes node is
.B Ready
and schedulable. Then it reboots the other workers, then the control plane,
then this node last. Each node must report ok before the next one starts,
followed by a pause of
.B ROLL_PAUSE
seconds. The rollout stops at the first node that does not come back healthy,
and does not start another node once
.B ROLL_MAX
seconds (default 4 hours) have passed.
Settling pauses and restore polling sleeps are capped to the remaining time.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-shutdown :
a cluster power-off, for a UPS or a power outage. The other workers are shut
down in parallel, then the control plane, then this node. Nodes are cordoned
but not drained. A node that is unreachable (probably already off) is skipped,
and a node that fails does not stop the others. For unattended use, add
.BR "\-y \-\-ignore-backups" .
.IP
With
.B \-\-status
or
.BR \-\-check ,
the action runs on every node.
.IP
Before a reboot or shutdown of the whole cluster,
.B safe-power
shows a blinking red warning that lists the nodes, and asks you to type
.BR yes .
.B \-y
skips the question but still shows the warning.
.TP
.B \-\-workers-only
With
.BR \-\-all :
leave the control plane out.
.TP
.BI \-\-max-time= MINUTES
With
.BR "\-\-reboot \-\-all" :
the time limit for the rollout (overrides
.BR ROLL_MAX ;
0 means no limit).
.TP
.B \-\-allow-concurrent
Skip the one-node-down lock.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show every step, including the backup findings and the container shutdown
plan, without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
Do not wait for a remote reboot to finish restoring.
.TP
.B \-\-wait\-backups
Wait for running backups to finish instead of aborting.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-backups
Continue even though a backup is running.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage.
.SH SETUP
.B safe-power-setup
.IR NODE ...
is run once, from any node, as an admin user who has SSH access and
.B sudo
on every node. It is safe to run again, for example to add a node. On every
node it:
.IP \(bu 2
installs
.BR safe-power ,
.B safe-power-setup
and this manual page;
.IP \(bu 2
writes
.BR /etc/safe-power/cluster.conf ;
.IP \(bu 2
creates the system account
.B safepower
with its password locked and an ed25519 key that has no passphrase;
.IP \(bu 2
installs a sudoers rule that lets
.B safepower
run only
.B /usr/local/sbin/safe-power
as root;
.IP \(bu 2
adds every node's key to
.BR authorized_keys ,
each with
.B restrict
and the forced command
.BR "safe-power \-\-ssh-gate" ,
and records every node's host key in
.BR known_hosts .
.PP
On the control plane it also creates the Kubernetes ServiceAccount
.BR kube-system/safe-power .
Its ClusterRole allows only what drain and uncordon need: nodes get/list/patch;
pods get/list/delete; pods/eviction create; and get/list on workload
controllers. The kubeconfig for it is copied to every node.
.PP
Options:
.BI \-\-control-plane " HOST" ,
.BI \-\-admin " USER" ,
.BI \-\-user " NAME" ,
.BI \-\-api-server " URL" ,
.BI \-\-ntfy " URL" ,
.BI \-\-ntfy-token " TOKEN" ,
.BI \-\-ha-webhook " URL" ,
.BR \-\-verify .
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
If
.B NOTIFY_NTFY_URL
or
.B NOTIFY_HA_WEBHOOK
is set,
.B safe-power
sends a notification when:
.IP \(bu 2
a node reboots, powers off or is drained;
.IP \(bu 2
a restore succeeds or fails;
.IP \(bu 2
a run is aborted (a backup is running, the drain failed, or another node is
down);
.IP \(bu 2
a rolling reboot or cluster shutdown starts, finishes or stops.
.PP
.B ntfy
gets a plain-text message with a title and a priority (urgent for failures).
.B NOTIFY_NTFY_TOKEN
adds a bearer token.
.B "Home Assistant"
gets a JSON POST to the webhook with the fields
.BR node ,
.B level
.RB ( info | ok | warn | fail ),
.B title
and
.BR message .
Notifications never fail a run, and are not sent during
.BR \-\-dry-run .
Failure messages do not expose notification URLs or tokens. JSON control
characters in Home Assistant messages are escaped.
Set the URLs cluster-wide with
.B safe-power-setup \-\-ntfy
.I URL
and
.B \-\-ha-webhook
.IR URL .
.BR \-\-ntfy-token
.IR TOKEN .
These settings are kept in
.BR /etc/safe-power/notify.conf ,
which is root-only (0600) on every node, because a webhook URL or ntfy topic
works like a password.
.SH SECURITY
The service account cannot open a shell. The SSH gate
.RB ( "safe-power \-\-ssh-gate" )
accepts only these commands:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR true ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B safe-power
with action flags only. A node argument is refused, so a remote call cannot
hop on to a third node;
.IP \(bu 2
.B kubectl get|drain|cordon|uncordon
with plain arguments.
.PP
Anything else is refused and logged to syslog under the tag
.BR safe-power .
.SH CONFIGURATION
The defaults are at the top of the script. They are overridden first by
.B /etc/safe-power/cluster.conf
(cluster layout, rewritten by
.BR safe-power-setup )
and then by
.B /etc/safe-power/local.conf
(your per-node overrides, never touched by setup). Both files are sourced as
bash.
.TP
.B CONTROL_PLANE_HOST, NODES, SVC_USER
Cluster layout (written by
.BR safe-power-setup ).
.TP
.B BACKUP_PROCS, BACKUP_CMDLINE, BACKUP_IGNORE, BACKUP_UNITS, BLOCKING_CONTAINERS, BACKUP_WAIT
Backup detection.
.TP
.B DB_IMAGES, INFRA_IMAGES, IMAGE_SUFFIXES
Container tiers.
.TP
.B STOP_TIMEOUT, DB_STOP_TIMEOUT, HEALTH_WAIT
Container stop and start timing.
.TP
.B DRAIN_TIMEOUT, CONVERGE_TIMEOUT, ROLL_PAUSE, ROLL_MAX
Drain, restore and rollout timing.
.TP
.B ONE_AT_A_TIME
1 (default) refuses to drain while another node is down.
.TP
.B NOTIFY_NTFY_URL, NOTIFY_NTFY_TOKEN, NOTIFY_HA_WEBHOOK
Notifications.
.TP
.B HEALTH_URLS
URLs that must answer 2xx/3xx after a restore (control plane only).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /var/log/safe-power.log
Log of every run on this node, without color codes: preflight findings,
drain, container stops and restore. A remote run writes to the log on the
target node, and the calling node logs its side too.
.TP
.B journalctl \-u safe-power-restore
Output of the restore that runs at boot (also appended to the log above).
.TP
.B /var/lib/safe-power/
State:
.B containers.txt
(tier and name of each container that was stopped),
.B drained_at
(present while the node is drained),
.B last_result
(read by
.BR \-\-status ),
.BR swarm_node_id .
.TP
.B /etc/safe-power/cluster.conf
Cluster layout. Identical on every node, and rewritten by
.BR safe-power-setup .
.TP
.B /etc/safe-power/notify.conf
Notification URLs and ntfy token (root-only, 0600). Rewritten by
.BR safe-power-setup .
.TP
.B /etc/safe-power/local.conf
Per-node overrides (root-only, 0600), for example
.BR HEALTH_URLS ,
.B BLOCKING_CONTAINERS
or
.BR BACKUP_WAIT .
.TP
.B /usr/local/share/man/man8/safe-power.8
This manual page.
.TP
.B /etc/safe-power/kubeconfig
Token for the
.B safe-power
Kubernetes ServiceAccount (root:safepower 0640).
.TP
.B /etc/sudoers.d/safe-power
The service account's single sudo rule.
.TP
.B /etc/systemd/system/safe-power-restore.service
Present only while a node is drained.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Aborted (a backup was running, apt was busy, another node was down, the drain
failed, the time limit was reached, or the user declined), or the restore
finished with problems.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 126
Command refused by the SSH gate.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview what a reboot of this node would do:
.B safe-power \-\-reboot \-\-dry-run
.TP
Reboot a worker from any node and wait until it is back:
.B safe-power \-\-reboot optiplex-three
.TP
Reboot tonight even if a backup is running, after waiting for it to finish:
.B safe-power \-\-reboot \-\-wait-backups \-y
.TP
Rolling reboot of the whole cluster:
.B safe-power \-\-reboot \-\-all
.TP
Rolling reboot of the workers only, with a 2-hour limit:
.B safe-power \-\-reboot \-\-all \-\-workers-only \-\-max-time=120
.TP
Power off the whole cluster from a UPS (NUT
.BR SHUTDOWNCMD ):
.B /usr/local/sbin/safe-power \-\-shutdown \-\-all \-y \-\-ignore-backups
.TP
Turn on phone notifications for every node:
.B safe-power-setup \-\-ntfy https://ntfy.sh/my-cluster
.TP
See every node's state:
.B safe-power \-\-status \-\-all
.TP
Always stop a container last:
.B docker run \-\-label safe-power.tier=3 ...
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kubectl-drain (1),
.BR docker-stop (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR sudoers (5),
.BR sshd (8)
